All endpoints should return consistent JSON responses and handle validation errors, missing data, provider failures, and rate limits.
11. Database requirements
Use Supabase PostgreSQL with the following initial tables:
profiles — user profile and preferred language.
saved_trips — flight number, origin, destination, travel date, user ID, and optional JSONB metadata.
chat_history — optional persistent conversations if required.
Enable Row Level Security for private records. Use the authenticated user's identity to enforce ownership.
12. Non-functional requirements
Responsive design: Usable on mobile, tablet, and desktop.
Performance: Load the dashboard quickly and fetch data only when needed.
Security: Store secret API keys in backend environment variables.
Reliability: Handle timeouts and API errors gracefully.
Accuracy: Do not invent flight status or aircraft positions.
Privacy: Keep saved trips private and minimize stored personal data.
Accessibility: Use readable contrast, labels, keyboard-accessible controls, and clear status text.
Maintainability: Separate flight-provider logic, AI logic, REST routes, and UI components.
13. MVP priorities
P0
Must be completed
Flight-number lookup, real flight status, aircraft position integration, map display, AI chat, responsive dashboard, loading and error states.
P1
Complete if time permits
Flight-offer search, Supabase authentication, saved trips, language selection.
P2
Future scope
Automated notifications, airport assistance, offline access, and personalized recommendations.
14. Team responsibilities
Team member
Responsibility
Person 1
React UI, dashboard, search forms, map interface, responsive design
Person 2
Express backend, flight APIs, Supabase, REST endpoints
Person 3
OpenAI assistant, multilingual support, integration testing, demo preparation

All team members should agree on the API response formats early. The frontend can use mock responses while backend integrations are being developed, then switch to the real endpoints.
15. Testing and acceptance criteria
The MVP is ready when:
Users can search for a flight and view returned status data.
Live aircraft coordinates display on the map when available.
Missing position data produces a clear fallback.
The AI assistant responds to questions using relevant flight context.
Multilingual responses work for the selected test languages.
Invalid searches and API failures do not crash the application.
Authentication and saved trips work if included.
Users cannot access other users' private trip records.
Secret keys are not exposed in frontend code or the GitHub repository.
The application works on desktop and mobile.
The demo clearly distinguishes real API data from mock data.
16. Demo plan
Open the AeroMate dashboard.
Search for a flight and show its status.
Open the aircraft tracking map and show a live position if available.
Ask the AI assistant to explain a delay.
Switch the response language.
Show saved trips if implemented.
Explain the architecture and demonstrate graceful handling of unavailable data.
17. Definition of done
AeroMate PS-01 is complete for the MVP when a traveler can search for a flight, view its latest available status, see aircraft position when supported, and receive practical AI travel guidance through a responsive web application.
The key priority is a working end-to-end experience, not the largest number of features. Build and test flight status first, connect aircraft tracking next, and then integrate the AI assistant. Add authentication and saved trips after the core flow is reliable.

